# <type>(<scope>): <summary>            <-- max ~72 chars, imperative ("add", not "added")
#
# <body: the WHY, not the what. Wrap at ~72. Optional for trivial changes.>
#
# Refs: #<issue>   ADR-<n>   F<n>/P<n>/T<n>   (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Conventional Commits — https://www.conventionalcommits.org
#
# type:   feat     a user-facing feature
#         fix      a bug fix
#         perf     a performance change
#         refactor code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature
#         test     adding or fixing tests
#         docs     documentation only
#         chore    tooling, deps, config, repo plumbing
#         style    formatting only (rare — the formatter usually does this)
#
# scope:  the area touched. Common scopes:
#         triage  search  vet  processor  serving  ml  frontend
#         docs    roadmap  ci  deps
#         (omit the scope if it spans many areas)
#
# Examples:
#   feat(vet): add per-candidate vetting CNN
#   fix(processor): read operating threshold from checkpoint (closes F3)
#   refactor(ml): promote windowing into spacesight_ml package (ADR-0011)
#   docs(roadmap): reorder R2 data-fix tasks
#
# Notes:
# - One logical change per commit. PRs squash-merge, so the squash summary
#   should also follow this format (it becomes the commit on main).
# - Reference findings (F/P/T) or ADRs in the trailer when a commit closes one,
#   so history is traceable back to the roadmap and decisions.
